{#- The base table, in EXCLUSIVE mode -- not the view, and not ACCESS
    EXCLUSIVE. Both halves of that are deliberate.

    The race being closed is a write that was validated against the old source
    and commits after the preflight has looked. Deferred foreign-key triggers
    make that window a real one: the check has already run by the time COMMIT
    comes round. EXCLUSIVE blocks every writer and no reader, which is exactly
    the shape of the problem -- and it catches writes that never went through
    the ORM, since every path into an overlay model lands here in the end.

    Locking the view instead would be the obvious move and the wrong one:
    Postgres locks the relations named in a view's definition along with it, so
    an ACCESS EXCLUSIVE lock on the view reaches through to the vendor's table
    and blocks every reader of it, everywhere, for the length of the cutover.
    The view still takes its own AccessExclusiveLock when CREATE OR REPLACE
    VIEW runs a moment later; that one is unavoidable and brief. -#}
LOCK TABLE {{ tenant_schema | qi }}.{{ base_table | qi }} IN EXCLUSIVE MODE
